art_direction.glass_treatment:
**One pill. Four words. Near-invisible until it isn't.** It is the only piece of chrome on the entire site — no header band, no footer bar, no scroll indicator — and that is what licenses it to be beautiful.

GEOMETRY
- **Desktop:** a floating pill, **auto-width capped at 560px**, height **52px**, centred, inset **20px** from the top. Contents, left to right: the OT monogram (26px tall, never the wordmark at nav scale) · a 1px hairline · `SHOP` · `DROP 01` · `STORY` · a 1px hairline · `BAG (2)`. Four words plus the mark. Every category — tees, polos, crewnecks, hoodies, shorts, sweatpants, jeans — lives behind SHOP, which opens a full-bleed panel. The wrapper is `pointer-events:none` with the pill `pointer-events:auto`, so the page behind stays clickable (Palace's trick).
- **Mobile:** the pill shrinks to **~232px wide, 54px tall**, centred, inset 14px. Contents: OT monogram · hairline · `MENU` · `BAG (2)`. This is not a compromise, it is the design *and* the performance fix: blurred-backdrop cost scales with area × dpr², so a 232×54 pill at dpr 3 costs roughly a tenth of a full-bleed bar. Each item carries a 44×44 hit area via negative margins inside the 54px shell.
- `border-radius: 2147483647px`. The site's radius policy is **binary**: everything is `0` except the two glass surfaces, which are maximum pills. No 8px, no 12px, no 16px, anywhere. Committing to both poles and refusing the middle is what stops it reading as a component library.

THERE IS EXACTLY ONE OTHER GLASS SURFACE ON THE SITE: the sticky filter bar on collection pages, `blur(10px)` over `--ot-paper` at 80%. That is the only place in the entire streetwear category where glass is actually used on a nav-adjacent element (Sporty & Rich), and it is used here for the same reason — it sits over a scrolling grid of product, which is the one backdrop where a blur genuinely helps. **No glass cards. No glass modals. One glass surface per viewport, ever.**

THE DEGRADATION LADDER — additive, and Tier 2 is the finished product

**Tier 3 — universal floor** (no `backdrop-filter` at all)
```css
.ot-pill{background:rgb(15 15 15 / .94);border:1px solid rgb(250 246 246 / .14);
  box-shadow:0 8px 32px -8px rgb(0 0 0 / .5)}
```

**Tier 2 — THE PRODUCT. Safari, Firefox, every phone.** Art-direct *this* until it is finished; add Tier 1 only if it still looks right when Tier 1 silently vanishes, which it will for most traffic.
```css
@supports ((backdrop-filter:blur(1px)) or (-webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(1px))){
 .ot-pill{
  /* scrim floor FIRST, glass tint over it */
  background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgb(250 246 246 / .10),rgb(250 246 246 / .03) 42%,
                             rgb(250 246 246 / .07)),
             rgb(15 15 15 / var(--ot-scrim,.78));
  -webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(16px) saturate(170%);
          backdrop-filter:blur(16px) saturate(170%);
  box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 0 rgb(250 246 246 / .38),
             inset 0 -1px 0 0 rgb(250 246 246 / .10),
             0 8px 32px -8px rgb(0 0 0 / .45);
  contain:paint; will-change:backdrop-filter}}
@media (hover:none) and (pointer:coarse){
 .ot-pill{-webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(12px) saturate(160%);
          backdrop-filter:blur(12px) saturate(160%)}}
```
`saturate(170%)` does more work than the blur — without it you get fog, not glass. Blur is capped at 16px because above ~20px low-end devices degrade sharply for no visible gain.

**Tier 1 — Chromium desktop garnish only.** Real refraction via `backdrop-filter: url(#ot-refract)`, driven by a **rounded-rect SDF → bevel → normal map** rendered to canvas and injected as a data-URI PNG (not `feTurbulence`, which gives a watery wobble that is wrong for a crisp pill). `filterUnits="userSpaceOnUse"`, `color-interpolation-filters="sRGB"` (non-optional — the SVG default is linearRGB and everything comes out washed), both `href` and `xlink:href` on `feImage`, filter region recomputed and map regenerated on resize. `bevel: 20px`, `scale: 42`. **No chromatic aberration** — RGB fringing reads as glitch and fights a washed, desaturated, vintage-athletic brand. Gate it with an engine check, not `@supports` (Firefox returns `true` and renders nothing): Chromium brands present, not an Apple platform, `min-width: 900px` and `pointer: fine`. On iOS every browser is WebKit, so there is no escape hatch and none is attempted.

ADAPTIVITY — this is the un-vibe-coded part, and it is load-bearing, not decorative
The pill flips its entire token set based on what section is under it, via one `IntersectionObserver` with `rootMargin` pinned to the pill's band (`-20px 0px -92% 0px`). This is not a flourish — **the maths forces it**: the dark scrim at .78 composited over the bone paper section yields a background luminance of ~0.21, against which bone ink is only **3.8:1** and fails. Flipped to a paper scrim at .70, navy ink lands at **11.6:1**. So the glass *must* know what it is over, or the nav is illegible on a third of the site.
```css
.ot-pill[data-over="dark"]{--ot-ink-nav:#FAF6F6;--ot-scrim:.78;--ot-rim:rgb(250 246 246 / .38)}
.ot-pill[data-over="light"]{--ot-ink-nav:#141B2C;--ot-scrim-color:rgb(250 246 246 / .70);
                            --ot-rim:rgb(250 246 246 / .85)}
.ot-pill{color:var(--ot-ink-nav);transition:color 200ms ease,background 200ms ease}
```
The **same observer** drives the second mechanism: over the cover video and over the pinned canvas sequence, the pill goes opaque. Blur over playing video or a per-frame canvas repaint means the backdrop is dirty every frame and the blur re-rasterises every frame — the single most expensive combination on any page, and worst on iOS where `position: fixed` + `backdrop-filter` already defeats WebKit's blur cache. The switch is an **opacity cross-fade between two pre-blurred layers** (240ms linear). **Never animate a blur radius** — a static blur can be cached, a transitioning one cannot.

ACCESSIBILITY — designed for the preference we cannot honour
`prefers-reduced-transparency` does not exist in Safari, which is precisely the platform whose users are most likely to have Reduce Transparency switched on, because Apple shipped it as the remedy for Liquid Glass legibility complaints. So it is wired, but it is not the protection. **The protection is the scrim floor** (.78 dark / .70 light, locked tokens, verified against both palette extremes) which means a user who gets *no* preference honoured is still above 4.5:1. `prefers-contrast: more` — which works everywhere including Safari 14.1+ — drops to fully opaque with a `currentColor` border. `forced-colors: active` drops to `Canvas`/`CanvasText`. `prefers-reduced-motion` freezes the specular. The 1px rim is never the only boundary cue.

Re-check WebKit bug 245510 and PR #68614 before ship; if refraction lands in Safari, Tier 1 becomes worth real investment and the engine gate needs rewriting. Until then it is a garnish, and nothing is missing without it.
